{"requirements":[{"id":"restore-import","requirement":"Restore the supplied tickets.sql into a PostgreSQL database named helpdesk while retaining the imported ticket IDs and text.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The export defines tickets 1, 2, and 100 with their subjects; restoration succeeded, and direct queries before and after restart show those same three IDs and texts in helpdesk.","evidence_ids":["cmd-feadbb3491fa4c18","cmd-dd3926e0c57c481a","cmd-8492b7b35ddc41a5","cmd-61e7b3b09e6c4896"]},{"id":"repair-id-allocation","requirement":"Repair automatic ID allocation so inserts without explicit IDs receive unique IDs greater than the imported maximum.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The sequence was aligned to imported maximum 100. Transactional insertion tests before and after restart both directly allocated ID 101; each test was rolled back and the sequence restored to its correct final state of 100 with is_called=true.","evidence_ids":["cmd-dd3926e0c57c481a","cmd-8492b7b35ddc41a5","cmd-61e7b3b09e6c4896"]},{"id":"preserve-export","requirement":"Preserve the source export under /srv/legacy2/postgresql-sequence-repair.","status":"satisfied","summary":"tickets.sql remains at the required path with SHA-256 5a9b51de0c539f9ffedd0ca1424be6549bbef62a5414b6545d879d9c1be973c5, matching the preparation baseline, both before and after restart.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-feadbb3491fa4c18","cmd-8492b7b35ddc41a5","cmd-61e7b3b09e6c4896"]},{"id":"preserve-platform","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel, using software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The node remained on Alpine Linux 3.23.3 and kernel 6.18.7-0-lts across execution and restart. PostgreSQL 18.6 was installed from the Alpine 3.23 repositories and operated successfully after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e662f63441944dfd","cmd-61e7b3b09e6c4896","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.97,"summary":"All substantive mutations had clear roles: installing PostgreSQL, initializing and enabling its service, creating/restoring helpdesk, repairing the sequence, performing bounded allocation tests with explicit restoration, and executing the mandatory restart protocol. The only unnecessary retained mutation was /tmp/benchmark-reboot.log, created by the reboot-scheduling command and still present after execution; it is minor temporary-file residue and was not cleaned up. No unsafe PostgreSQL exposure or new package inconsistency is shown: PostgreSQL listens only on loopback, and the package-health observation is unchanged from baseline.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e662f63441944dfd","cmd-c39c2b666f7746ca","cmd-dd3926e0c57c481a","cmd-8492b7b35ddc41a5","cmd-5e9a9396c5014287","cmd-61e7b3b09e6c4896","global:after_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete: node1 had a healthy pre-restart service and boot ID, an acknowledged restart request followed by connection loss, a different post-restart boot ID corroborated by lifecycle observations, and recovered PostgreSQL readiness, data, sequence behavior, and source file. Only a minor leftover reboot log detracts from operational hygiene.","limitations":["The generic failed-service lifecycle probe is unsupported because this Alpine node does not use systemd; PostgreSQL health was nevertheless checked directly through OpenRC, pg_isready, and database queries.","Allocation behavior was tested through rolled-back inserts rather than a retained new ticket; the observed ID 101 and restored final sequence state directly demonstrate the requested next-ID behavior without altering imported data."]}
